The price difference between Blue Nile and James Allen at 1ct G-VS2 is typically $100–$300 — less than 10%. Both use GIA certification and HD 360° video. The practical choice between them often comes down to which site's search tools you find easier to use and which has the specific stone at the moment you are ready to buy.

Brilliant Earth charges a premium above Blue Nile — approximately 8–15% more for equivalent grade — tied to their ethical sourcing narrative and in-store appointment option. For buyers who want sourcing transparency, that premium is justified. For buyers who prioritize GIA certification and price, Blue Nile and James Allen are equivalent.

Whiteflash and Brian Gavin sell super-ideal cut stones — a tier above GIA Excellent that maximizes light return using proprietary cut grading systems. These cost 15–30% more than Blue Nile's Excellent rounds. For buyers who want the best-performing stone regardless of price, super-ideal is a legitimate category.

For most buyers, GIA Excellent at Blue Nile is indistinguishable in real-world wear.

