## The 1ct Carat Weight Threshold: Why 1.00ct Costs More Than 0.99ct

A 1.00ct diamond is not just 1% heavier than a 0.99ct diamond. It costs roughly 15–20% more per carat. This is the carat threshold effect — a systematic price premium at round numbers (0.50ct, 0.75ct, 1.00ct, 1.50ct, 2.00ct) driven by buyer demand, not by any meaningful quality difference.

The face-up size difference between a 0.99ct and a 1.00ct GIA Excellent round is approximately 0.03mm in diameter — invisible to the human eye in any normal setting.

This is why the 0.90ct strategy exists. At 0.90ct, you are below the 1ct threshold and below the incremental premium steps between 0.90 and 0.99ct. A 0.90ct G-VS1 at $2,487 delivers a 6.3mm diameter stone.

A 1.00ct G-VS2 at $3,230 delivers a 6.5mm diameter stone. The 0.2mm difference costs $743.

**When to pay the 1ct premium:**

Pay it if the certificate number matters to the recipient — some partners specifically want a "one carat" diamond and the certificate to match. Pay it if resale value is a consideration — 1ct diamonds have higher secondary market liquidity than sub-carat stones. Pay it if the total budget is comfortable above $3,500 for the ring.

**When to take the 0.90ct instead:**

Take the 0.90ct when budget is the primary constraint, when the recipient has not specifically requested a 1ct stone, or when the $700+ savings funds a meaningfully better setting or higher clarity grade. Most buyers who see both stones in rings cannot identify which is which.

The 0.90ct strategy works particularly well when paired with a VS1 upgrade. A 0.90ct G-VS1 at $2,487 has cleaner inclusions than a 1ct G-VS2 at $3,230 — and the 0.90ct is $743 less. You get a higher clarity grade in a visually identical size for a lower price.

That is the most efficient path through the 1ct price tier.

