## The 5-Shape Audit: What This Methodology Covers

Every price in this post comes from live Blue Nile inventory pulled in June–July 2026. The audit methodology — which I call The 5-Shape Audit — pulls the lowest-priced stone at each target grade from the active inventory, notes the sweet-spot range, and checks whether the certification standard (GIA for rounds and princess, GIA/IGI for fancy shapes) is met.

The 5-Shape Audit does not average prices. It pulls specific stones with real SKU numbers so you can verify them directly. This is different from most diamond price guides that use hypothetical price ranges from market averages.

If a stone I listed has sold, the link will redirect you to a comparable replacement — Blue Nile's system shows similar stones automatically.

**What the audit does not cover:**

Blue Nile also sells preset engagement rings (diamond set in a specific band before you buy), designer collections, and halo presets. These are not covered here. The preset market is a separate buying decision — this audit is strictly for buyers choosing their own diamond and setting separately, which is the approach that produces the best value at every budget.

**How frequently prices change:**

Blue Nile's diamond prices change daily in response to rough diamond market conditions, currency movements, and inventory turnover. The prices in this audit were accurate on the date listed. By the time you read this, specific stone prices may have shifted $50–$300 in either direction.

Use the prices here as a calibration, not a guarantee.

**Why GIA for rounds?**

Blue Nile stocks GIA-certified rounds across their natural inventory. GIA is the most trusted and widely recognized diamond grading laboratory — their color and clarity grades are the most conservative and consistent in the industry. When a stone carries a GIA report, the grade you read on the certificate is the grade you can rely on.

This matters at 1ct because the price difference between G and H, or VS2 and VS1, can be $200–$500 on a single stone. You want that grading to be trustworthy.

